(* Sharepoint protocol 2, the first link (XXhfs) under an active attacker in
   the middle: what the six-digit code buys.

   The code is sas(h2, nc): h2 the handshake hash after message 2, nc the
   client's nonce, committed to in message 1 and revealed in message 3. Six
   digits are short, so the attacker is given a collision oracle: knowing one
   side's (h2, nc) and the other side's h2, coll() makes a nonce that gives the
   other side the same code. That is the power a real attacker has by search
   (10^6 tries). What stops it is order: the server's nonce is fixed by the
   commitment before the server's ephemeral (and so its h2) exists, and the
   client's h2 is fixed before its nonce leaves (handshake_nonce.pv).
   code_order.pv is the sanity check: on the handshake's skeleton, removing
   the commitment check lets ProVerif find the attack with this oracle (this
   full model without the check does not finish).

   The person reading the receiver's screen is an authentic channel (`screen`);
   the code is also public once shown. Statics are one key per machine for the
   whole run (the engine makes one at start), as in the code; nothing trusts
   them. Expected: every query true (about 5 minutes on an M-series Mac). *)

free fileA, probeA: bitstring [private].

(* The sender's person typed the code the receiver showed: both ran the same
   handshake (same h2, nonce, keys and resume secret), one for one. *)
query h: bitstring, n: bitstring, k1: bitstring, k2: bitstring, r: bitstring;
  inj-event(ClientAccepts(h, n, k1, k2, r)) ==> inj-event(ServerShows(h, n, k1, k2, r)).
(* What goes after the code (the offer, the files) and the resume secret stay secret. *)
query attacker(fileA).
query attacker(probeA).
process
  new sc: bitstring; new ss: bitstring;
  out(c, (exp(g, sc), exp(g, ss)));
  !( new screen: channel;
     ( XXClient(c, c, screen, sc, false, false, fileA, probeA)
     | XXServer(c, c, screen, ss, false, true) ) )
